saveOutputs: false
cells:
  - kind: 1
    value: "# 11i · Evaluation – What does each building block add to the result?\n\nJonas got a good answer because several building blocks worked together. Was that luck? The test set contains all knee reports with a known diagnosis. Each method has to diagnose them without seeing the answer – first every building block alone, then in combinations.\n\n**Question:** How accurate is the diagnosis with vector search, full text, ontology, GraphRAG and spatial – alone and combined?\n\n- **Features:** AI Vector Search, Oracle Text, RDF knowledge graph (`SEM_MATCH`), GraphRAG in SQL, Spatial with OpenStreetMap tags (JSON), SQL property graph, optional LLM via `DBMS_VECTOR_CHAIN`\n- **Pattern:** M1–M4 measured: which combination gives the best answers?\n- **Data:** test set: all FHIR symptom reports with a knee injury diagnosis (`kniedistorsion`, `vkb_ruptur`, `innenmeniskusläsion`, `innenbandzerrung`, `knieprellung`), about 370 reports; the diagnosis in `DIAGNOSES` is the ground truth\n- **Run:** as the demo user (DOAG2026) in SQL Developer for VS Code, after `01_run_all.sql` with the regenerated data (data/_generator/knee_cases.py). Run the cells from top to bottom.\n\n**Important for the slides:** the data is synthetic. The generator writes knee reports so that each building block can find a different kind of signal: knowledge-graph terms, word variants for the full-text index, paraphrases, and the location of app reports. The notebook therefore shows *how* the building blocks complement each other on demo data. It does not prove accuracy for real medical data. 5 % of the labels are deliberately wrong, so no method can reach 100 %.\n\nThe notebook creates its working tables with the prefix `EVAL_` (re-runnable: each table is dropped and created again). Section 7 needs an OCI Generative AI credential and is optional.\n\n> Fictitious demo: no medical device and no medical advice. Places from OpenStreetMap (© OpenStreetMap contributors, ODbL); all persons and story places are synthetic."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "### Session settings\n\nThe story takes place on Monday, 16 November 2026, in Franconia. With the session time zone `Europe/Berlin`, all timestamps and literals read in local time.\n\nThe SQLcl settings in the second cell make the text output readable: columns as wide as their content instead of their declared length (`SQLFORMAT ansiconsole`; values from JSON are `VARCHAR2(4000)`), one header per result (`PAGESIZE`), wider lines (`LINESIZE`) and complete JSON documents and CLOBs (`LONG`, `LONGCHUNKSIZE`)."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "ALTER SESSION SET TIME_ZONE = 'Europe/Berlin';"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "SET SQLFORMAT ansiconsole\nSET PAGESIZE 1000\nSET LINESIZE 250\nSET LONG 1000000\nSET LONGCHUNKSIZE 250"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## The methods\n\nNine methods compete, from a simple baseline to all building blocks together.\n\n**Question:** Which methods are compared, and how is success measured?\n\n**How the query answers it:** Every method predicts one of the five knee diagnoses for each report in the test set. The report itself is never part of its own evidence (leave one out).\n\n| # | Method | Building blocks | How it decides |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| M0 | Majority | – | always the most frequent diagnosis (baseline) |\n| M1 | Vector | embeddings | the 10 most similar reports (`VECTOR_DISTANCE`) vote |\n| M2 | Full text | text index | the 10 best reports for an `ACCUM` query of all words of the report vote |\n| M3 | Hybrid | embeddings + text index | both lists of 10 vote together |\n| M4 | Ontology | knowledge graph, exact match | findings = KG terms found literally in the text (`INSTR`), then the injury patterns as rules |\n| M5 | Ontology + full text | knowledge graph + text index | findings = KG terms found with `CONTAINS` and German stemming, then the rules |\n| M6 | GraphRAG | embeddings + knowledge graph + text index | vector votes, plus: a pattern that fits adds 1.5, a counter-indicator subtracts 1 |\n| M7 | GraphRAG + Spatial | M6 + location | plus 0.3 if the terrain at the report location is the typical terrain of the pattern |\n| M8 | All | embeddings + text index + knowledge graph + spatial | hybrid votes instead of vector votes, otherwise as M7 |\n\nRules (M4, M5) only answer when a pattern fits; otherwise they abstain, and the report counts as wrong.\n\n**Metrics:** accuracy, coverage (share of reports with an answer), recall for cruciate ligament ruptures (`vkb_ruptur`: a missed rupture means wrong advice) and macro recall (mean recall over the five diagnoses)."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 1. Settings\n\nThe combined methods weigh different kinds of evidence against each other.\n\n**Question:** Which weights are used?\n\n**How the query answers it:** The weights of the combined methods are kept in a small table, so they can be changed and the predictions (section 5) calculated again without editing the queries."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "DROP TABLE IF EXISTS eval_settings PURGE;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "CREATE TABLE eval_settings (\n  param    VARCHAR2(30)  NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY,\n  value    NUMBER        NOT NULL,\n  meaning  VARCHAR2(200)\n);"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "INSERT INTO eval_settings (param, value, meaning) VALUES\n  ('w_fit',          1.5, 'score for a diagnosis whose injury pattern fits the findings'),\n  ('w_against',      1.0, 'penalty for a diagnosis whose pattern has a counter-indicator in the report'),\n  ('w_terrain',      0.3, 'score for a diagnosis whose typical terrain matches the report location'),\n  ('trail_distance', 30,  'maximum distance in metres between report location and OSM path'),\n  ('feasible_km',    30,  'maximum straight-line distance to the radiology for a feasible recommendation');"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "COMMIT;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 2. The test set and the location context\n\nThe test set is every report whose diagnosis we know. About half of them come from the app and know where the injury happened.\n\n**Question:** Which reports are tested, and in which terrain did they happen?\n\n**How the query answers it:**\n\n`EVAL_CASES` holds every FHIR symptom report with one of the five knee diagnoses. The diagnosis is the ground truth.\n\nAbout half of the reports come from the app and have a location (FHIR geolocation extension, loaded into `SYMPTOM_REPORTS.GEOM`). The location becomes a **terrain** in two steps (pattern M3):\n\n1. `SDO_WITHIN_DISTANCE` finds the nearest OpenStreetMap way within 30 m in `OSM_TRAILS`. Its JSON tags decide: `highway` = `path` or `steps`, or `sac_scale` from `mountain_hiking` upwards → *DemandingPath*; otherwise → *EasyPath*.\n2. No way nearby, but inside the OSM city boundaries of Nuremberg or Fürth (`SDO_ANYINTERACT`) → *Town*."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "DROP TABLE IF EXISTS eval_cases PURGE;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "CREATE TABLE eval_cases AS\nSELECT s.report_id,\n       d.diagnosis_code               AS true_code,\n       CAST(NULL AS VARCHAR2(20))     AS terrain\nFROM   symptom_reports s\nJOIN   diagnoses d ON d.report_id = s.report_id\nWHERE  s.source = 'fhir'\nAND    d.diagnosis_code IN ('kniedistorsion', 'vkb_ruptur', 'innenmeniskusläsion', 'innenbandzerrung', 'knieprellung');"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "ALTER TABLE eval_cases ADD CONSTRAINT pk_eval_cases PRIMARY KEY (report_id);"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "UPDATE eval_cases e\nSET    e.terrain = (\n         SELECT CASE WHEN JSON_VALUE(t.tags, '$.highway') IN ('path', 'steps')\n                       OR JSON_VALUE(t.tags, '$.sac_scale') IN ('mountain_hiking', 'demanding_mountain_hiking', 'alpine_hiking')\n                     THEN 'DemandingPath' ELSE 'EasyPath' END\n         FROM   symptom_reports s\n         JOIN   osm_trails t\n           ON   SDO_WITHIN_DISTANCE(t.geom, s.geom, 'distance=30 unit=M') = 'TRUE'\n         WHERE  s.report_id = e.report_id\n         AND    t.osm_type  = 'way'\n         ORDER  BY SDO_GEOM.SDO_DISTANCE(t.geom, s.geom, 0.05, 'unit=M')\n         FETCH  FIRST 1 ROW ONLY)\nWHERE  e.report_id IN (SELECT s.report_id FROM symptom_reports s WHERE s.geom IS NOT NULL);"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "UPDATE eval_cases e\nSET    e.terrain = 'Town'\nWHERE  e.terrain IS NULL\nAND    EXISTS (SELECT 1\n               FROM   symptom_reports s\n               JOIN   osm_boundaries b ON SDO_ANYINTERACT(b.geom, s.geom) = 'TRUE'\n               WHERE  s.report_id = e.report_id\n               AND    b.name IN ('Nürnberg', 'Fürth'));"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "COMMIT;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "### Test set by diagnosis and terrain\n\n**Question:** How are diagnoses and terrains distributed?\n\n**How the query answers it:** Sprains are the most frequent diagnosis. Cruciate ligament ruptures happen more often on demanding paths, sprains on easy tracks, contusions in town – the signal the spatial part can use. *(no location)* = report from a practice.\n\n**Result:** 360 knee reports with a known diagnosis form the test set; 222 of them have a location. The terrain signal is clear: 34 of the 52 cruciate ligament ruptures with a location happened on a demanding path, 53 of the 79 sprains with a location on an easy track. Contusions (25 of 28) and meniscus lesions (24 of 32) are mostly reported from town. The sum row is labelled *all*."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT CASE WHEN GROUPING(true_code) = 1 THEN 'all' ELSE true_code END AS true_code,\n       COUNT(*)                                                     AS reports,\n       COUNT(CASE WHEN terrain = 'DemandingPath' THEN 1 END)        AS demanding_path,\n       COUNT(CASE WHEN terrain = 'EasyPath'      THEN 1 END)        AS easy_track,\n       COUNT(CASE WHEN terrain = 'Town'          THEN 1 END)        AS town,\n       COUNT(CASE WHEN terrain IS NULL           THEN 1 END)        AS no_location\nFROM   eval_cases\nGROUP  BY ROLLUP (true_code)\nORDER  BY GROUPING(true_code), reports DESC, true_code;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 3. The knowledge graph as tables\n\nThe rules of the evaluation come from the knowledge graph, not from the evaluation code.\n\n**Question:** Which terms, patterns and indicators does the graph provide?\n\n**How the query answers it:**\n\n`SEM_MATCH` reads the knee knowledge graph once into three working tables, so the evaluation runs as plain SQL:\n\n- `EVAL_KG_TERMS`: findings and mechanisms with all German labels and synonyms (`rdfs:label`, `skos:altLabel`)\n- `EVAL_KG_PATTERNS`: injury patterns with diagnosis code, minimum number of strong indicators, required mechanism, typical terrain and urgency\n- `EVAL_KG_INDICATORS`: strong indicators and counter-indicators per pattern\n\nLabels with brackets or slashes (e.g. *Rasche Schwellung (unter 2 Stunden)*) are descriptions, not words a patient writes; they are left out."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "DROP TABLE IF EXISTS eval_kg_terms PURGE;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "CREATE TABLE eval_kg_terms AS\nSELECT REGEXP_SUBSTR(k.term, '[^#]+$')                                   AS term,\n       CASE WHEN k.cls = 'https://doag2026.example/knee#Finding' THEN 'finding' ELSE 'mechanism' END AS kind,\n       CAST(k.label AS VARCHAR2(200))                                    AS label\nFROM   TABLE(SEM_MATCH(\n         'PREFIX kg:   <https://doag2026.example/knee#>\n            PREFIX rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>\n            PREFIX skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#>\n            SELECT ?term ?cls ?label\n            WHERE {\n              ?term a ?cls .\n              FILTER (?cls IN (kg:Finding, kg:Mechanism))\n              { ?term rdfs:label ?label } UNION { ?term skos:altLabel ?label }\n              FILTER (lang(?label) = \"de\")\n            }',\n         SEM_MODELS('KNEE_KG'), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, ' ', NULL, NULL,\n         'DOAG2026', 'RDF_NETWORK')) k\nWHERE  INSTR(k.label, '(') = 0\nAND    INSTR(k.label, '/') = 0;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "DROP TABLE IF EXISTS eval_kg_patterns PURGE;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "CREATE TABLE eval_kg_patterns AS\nSELECT REGEXP_SUBSTR(k.pattern, '[^#]+$')    AS pattern,\n       CAST(k.diagnosis_code AS VARCHAR2(60)) AS diagnosis_code,\n       TO_NUMBER(k.min_strong)               AS min_strong,\n       REGEXP_SUBSTR(k.mechanism, '[^#]+$')  AS mechanism,\n       REGEXP_SUBSTR(k.terrain, '[^#]+$')    AS terrain,\n       TO_NUMBER(k.urgency_hours)            AS urgency_hours\nFROM   TABLE(SEM_MATCH(\n         'PREFIX kg:   <https://doag2026.example/knee#>\n            PREFIX rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>\n            PREFIX skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#>\n            SELECT ?pattern ?diagnosis_code ?min_strong ?mechanism ?terrain ?urgency_hours\n            WHERE {\n              ?pattern a kg:InjuryPattern ;\n                       kg:suggests            ?injury ;\n                       kg:minStrongIndicators ?min_strong ;\n                       kg:requiresMechanism   ?mechanism ;\n                       kg:urgencyHours        ?urgency_hours .\n              ?injury kg:caseDiagnosisCode ?diagnosis_code .\n              OPTIONAL { ?pattern kg:typicalTerrain ?terrain }\n            }',\n         SEM_MODELS('KNEE_KG'), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, ' ', NULL, NULL,\n         'DOAG2026', 'RDF_NETWORK')) k;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "DROP TABLE IF EXISTS eval_kg_indicators PURGE;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "CREATE TABLE eval_kg_indicators AS\nSELECT REGEXP_SUBSTR(k.pattern, '[^#]+$') AS pattern,\n       REGEXP_SUBSTR(k.finding, '[^#]+$') AS finding,\n       CAST('strong' AS VARCHAR2(10))     AS role\nFROM   TABLE(SEM_MATCH(\n         'PREFIX kg:   <https://doag2026.example/knee#>\n            PREFIX rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>\n            PREFIX skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#>\n            SELECT ?pattern ?finding WHERE { ?pattern kg:hasStrongIndicator ?finding }',\n         SEM_MODELS('KNEE_KG'), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, ' ', NULL, NULL,\n         'DOAG2026', 'RDF_NETWORK')) k\nUNION ALL\nSELECT REGEXP_SUBSTR(k.pattern, '[^#]+$'),\n       REGEXP_SUBSTR(k.finding, '[^#]+$'),\n       'against'\nFROM   TABLE(SEM_MATCH(\n         'PREFIX kg:   <https://doag2026.example/knee#>\n            PREFIX rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>\n            PREFIX skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#>\n            SELECT ?pattern ?finding WHERE { ?pattern kg:argumentAgainst ?finding }',\n         SEM_MODELS('KNEE_KG'), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, ' ', NULL, NULL,\n         'DOAG2026', 'RDF_NETWORK')) k;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "### The rules from the graph\n\n**Question:** What does each injury pattern require?\n\n**How the query answers it:** One row per pattern: what it needs, what argues against it and where it typically happens. The sprain pattern is a diagnosis of exclusion: it needs both unspecific signs and must not show any sign of a more specific injury.\n\n**Result:** five patterns, the same rules as in 11e. The cruciate ligament rupture needs a twist and 2 of 3 strong indicators (pop, giving way, rapid swelling) and has no counter-indicator. The sprain needs mild swelling and pain when walking and has six counter-indicators – every sign of the other patterns. Each pattern also names its typical terrain: demanding path for rupture and medial ligament strain, easy track for the sprain, town for meniscus lesion and contusion. Urgency ranges from 48 hours (rupture) to 336 hours (sprain). Nothing of this is in the evaluation code – change the graph, and the evaluation changes with it."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT p.pattern,\n       p.diagnosis_code,\n       p.min_strong,\n       p.mechanism,\n       p.terrain,\n       p.urgency_hours,\n       (SELECT LISTAGG(i.finding, ', ') WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY i.finding)\n        FROM   eval_kg_indicators i\n        WHERE  i.pattern = p.pattern AND i.role = 'strong')  AS strong_indicators,\n       (SELECT LISTAGG(i.finding, ', ') WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY i.finding)\n        FROM   eval_kg_indicators i\n        WHERE  i.pattern = p.pattern AND i.role = 'against') AS counter_indicators\nFROM   eval_kg_patterns p\nORDER  BY p.urgency_hours, p.pattern;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 4. Evidence per report\n\nEach building block looks at different evidence in the same report.\n\n**Question:** What evidence does each building block collect?\n\n**How the query answers it:** ### 4a. Findings: exact match versus full-text index\n\n`EVAL_FINDINGS` stores which KG terms each report contains, found in two ways:\n\n- **exact:** the label occurs literally in the text (`INSTR` on lower case). This is what an application without a text index would do.\n- **text:** the text index searches for the label: every word with the German stem operator `$`, all words of a label with `AND`, the labels of a term with `OR`. Stop words of `SYMPTOM_STOPLIST` are left out. This also finds *\"Beim Gehen tut es weh\"* for *\"tut beim Gehen weh\"*.\n\nThe generated `CONTAINS` queries are kept in `EVAL_TERM_QUERIES`."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "DROP TABLE IF EXISTS eval_findings PURGE;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "CREATE TABLE eval_findings (\n  extraction  VARCHAR2(10) NOT NULL,\n  report_id   VARCHAR2(30) NOT NULL,\n  term        VARCHAR2(60) NOT NULL,\n  CONSTRAINT pk_eval_findings PRIMARY KEY (extraction, report_id, term)\n);"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "DROP TABLE IF EXISTS eval_term_queries PURGE;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "CREATE TABLE eval_term_queries (\n  term            VARCHAR2(60)   NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY,\n  contains_query  VARCHAR2(4000)\n);"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "INSERT INTO eval_findings (extraction, report_id, term)\nSELECT DISTINCT 'exact', e.report_id, t.term\nFROM   eval_cases e\nJOIN   symptom_reports s ON s.report_id = e.report_id\nJOIN   eval_kg_terms t   ON INSTR(LOWER(s.report_text), LOWER(t.label)) > 0;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "DECLARE\n  l_query  VARCHAR2(4000);\n  l_label  VARCHAR2(1000);\nBEGIN\n  FOR t IN (SELECT DISTINCT term FROM eval_kg_terms ORDER BY term) LOOP\n    l_query := NULL;\n    FOR l IN (SELECT label FROM eval_kg_terms WHERE term = t.term ORDER BY label) LOOP\n      l_label := NULL;\n      -- words of the label without stop words, each with the German stem operator\n      FOR w IN (SELECT x.word\n                FROM   (SELECT LOWER(REGEXP_SUBSTR(l.label, '[[:alpha:]]+', 1, LEVEL)) AS word, LEVEL AS pos\n                        FROM   dual\n                        CONNECT BY REGEXP_SUBSTR(l.label, '[[:alpha:]]+', 1, LEVEL) IS NOT NULL) x\n                WHERE  LENGTH(x.word) >= 3\n                AND    NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM ctx_user_stopwords sw\n                                   WHERE  sw.spw_stoplist = 'SYMPTOM_STOPLIST'\n                                   AND    LOWER(sw.spw_word) = x.word)\n                ORDER  BY x.pos) LOOP\n        l_label := l_label || CASE WHEN l_label IS NOT NULL THEN ' AND ' END || '$' || w.word;\n      END LOOP;\n      IF l_label IS NOT NULL THEN\n        l_query := l_query || CASE WHEN l_query IS NOT NULL THEN ' OR ' END || '(' || l_label || ')';\n      END IF;\n    END LOOP;\n\n    INSERT INTO eval_term_queries (term, contains_query) VALUES (t.term, l_query);\n    IF l_query IS NOT NULL THEN\n      INSERT INTO eval_findings (extraction, report_id, term)\n      SELECT 'text', e.report_id, t.term\n      FROM   eval_cases e\n      JOIN   symptom_reports s ON s.report_id = e.report_id\n      WHERE  CONTAINS(s.report_text, l_query) > 0;\n    END IF;\n  END LOOP;\n  COMMIT;\nEND;\n/"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "### What the text index adds\n\n**Question:** How many more findings does the text index find with the same knowledge?\n\n**How the query answers it:** Per KG term: the generated `CONTAINS` query and in how many reports each extraction finds the term. The text index finds clearly more – the same knowledge, but word order, inflection and extra words no longer matter.\n\n**Result:** for most terms the text index finds 1.5 to 2 times as many reports: *PainOnWalking* 192 instead of 97, *MildSwelling* 139 instead of 83, *RapidSwelling* 38 instead of 21, *GivingWay* 45 instead of 29. Where the patients use exactly the labels of the graph – *AudiblePop*, *Locking*, *Bruise* – both find the same. *Supination* (ankle), *Overuse* and *AnteriorKneePain* (patellofemoral pain) belong to no knee pattern of the test set and find (almost) nothing.\n\nTwo limits stay visible: the index does not read negations – *\"Kein Plopp\"* still counts as *AudiblePop* – and a short label such as *gibt* (from *gibt nach*) is broad. Both are part of the comparison: M5 shows what the index gains and what it costs."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT q.term,\n       q.contains_query,\n       COUNT(CASE WHEN f.extraction = 'exact' THEN 1 END) AS reports_exact,\n       COUNT(CASE WHEN f.extraction = 'text'  THEN 1 END) AS reports_text_index\nFROM   eval_term_queries q\nLEFT   JOIN eval_findings f ON f.term = q.term\nGROUP  BY q.term, q.contains_query\nORDER  BY reports_text_index DESC;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "### 4b. Neighbours: vector search and full-text search\n\n**Question:** Which earlier reports are most similar – by meaning and by words?\n\n**How the query answers it:**\n\n`EVAL_NEIGHBORS` stores the 10 most similar *other* FHIR reports with a diagnosis, once per retrieval:\n\n- **vector:** `VECTOR_DISTANCE(…, COSINE)` to the report's embedding; `OUTER APPLY` runs the nearest-neighbour search once per report.\n- **text:** a PL/SQL loop builds an `ACCUM` query from the words of each report (stem operator, without stop words and short words) and keeps the 10 reports with the highest `SCORE`. This is the full-text counterpart of a similarity search (\"more like this\").\n\nThe vector part takes a few seconds, the full-text part about a minute.\n\n**Result:** both retrievals deliver exactly 10 neighbours for each of the 360 reports – 3,600 rows each. No report is left without neighbours, so M1 to M3 vote on the same footing."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "DROP TABLE IF EXISTS eval_neighbors PURGE;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "CREATE TABLE eval_neighbors (\n  retrieval      VARCHAR2(10) NOT NULL,\n  report_id      VARCHAR2(30) NOT NULL,\n  rank_no        NUMBER       NOT NULL,\n  neighbor_id    VARCHAR2(30) NOT NULL,\n  neighbor_code  VARCHAR2(60) NOT NULL,\n  score          NUMBER,\n  CONSTRAINT pk_eval_neighbors PRIMARY KEY (retrieval, report_id, rank_no)\n);"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "INSERT INTO eval_neighbors (retrieval, report_id, rank_no, neighbor_id, neighbor_code, score)\nSELECT 'vector', q.report_id, n.rank_no, n.report_id, n.diagnosis_code, n.distance\nFROM   eval_cases e\nJOIN   symptom_reports q ON q.report_id = e.report_id\nOUTER  APPLY (SELECT s.report_id,\n                     d.diagnosis_code,\n                     VECTOR_DISTANCE(s.report_embedding, q.report_embedding, COSINE) AS distance,\n                     ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY VECTOR_DISTANCE(s.report_embedding, q.report_embedding, COSINE),\n                                                 s.report_id) AS rank_no\n              FROM   symptom_reports s\n              JOIN   diagnoses d ON d.report_id = s.report_id\n              WHERE  s.source = 'fhir'\n              AND    s.report_id <> q.report_id\n              ORDER  BY distance, s.report_id\n              FETCH  FIRST 10 ROWS ONLY) n;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "DECLARE\n  l_query  VARCHAR2(4000);\nBEGIN\n  FOR c IN (SELECT e.report_id, s.report_text\n            FROM   eval_cases e\n            JOIN   symptom_reports s ON s.report_id = e.report_id) LOOP\n    l_query := NULL;\n    FOR w IN (SELECT DISTINCT x.word\n              FROM   (SELECT LOWER(REGEXP_SUBSTR(c.report_text, '[[:alpha:]]+', 1, LEVEL)) AS word\n                      FROM   dual\n                      CONNECT BY REGEXP_SUBSTR(c.report_text, '[[:alpha:]]+', 1, LEVEL) IS NOT NULL) x\n              WHERE  LENGTH(x.word) >= 4\n              AND    x.word NOT IN ('about', 'accum', 'equiv', 'fuzzy', 'haspath', 'inpath', 'mdata', 'minus', 'near', 'within')\n              AND    NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM ctx_user_stopwords sw\n                                 WHERE  sw.spw_stoplist = 'SYMPTOM_STOPLIST'\n                                 AND    LOWER(sw.spw_word) = x.word)) LOOP\n      l_query := l_query || CASE WHEN l_query IS NOT NULL THEN ' ACCUM ' END || '$' || w.word;\n    END LOOP;\n\n    IF l_query IS NOT NULL THEN\n      INSERT INTO eval_neighbors (retrieval, report_id, rank_no, neighbor_id, neighbor_code, score)\n      SELECT 'text', c.report_id, ROWNUM, h.report_id, h.diagnosis_code, h.score\n      FROM   (SELECT s.report_id, d.diagnosis_code, SCORE(1) AS score\n              FROM   symptom_reports s\n              JOIN   diagnoses d ON d.report_id = s.report_id\n              WHERE  s.source = 'fhir'\n              AND    s.report_id <> c.report_id\n              AND    CONTAINS(s.report_text, l_query, 1) > 0\n              ORDER  BY SCORE(1) DESC, s.report_id\n              FETCH  FIRST 10 ROWS ONLY) h;\n    END IF;\n  END LOOP;\n  COMMIT;\nEND;\n/"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT retrieval,\n       COUNT(DISTINCT report_id) AS reports,\n       COUNT(*)                  AS neighbours\nFROM   eval_neighbors\nGROUP  BY retrieval\nORDER  BY retrieval;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "### 4c. Rule check per report and pattern\n\n**Question:** Which injury patterns fit each report?\n\n**How the query answers it:** The view `EVAL_RULE_CHECK_V` applies the injury patterns to the findings of each report, once per extraction (exact, text). It only looks at pairs of report and pattern where at least one indicator of the pattern was found – a pattern without any indicator can neither fit nor be contradicted, and no cross product is needed.\n\nA pattern **fits** when the report has at least `min_strong` strong indicators, no counter-indicator, and either names no mechanism or names the mechanism the pattern requires."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW eval_rule_check_v AS\nWITH pairs AS (\n  SELECT DISTINCT f.extraction, f.report_id, i.pattern\n  FROM   eval_findings f\n  JOIN   eval_kg_indicators i ON i.finding = f.term\n),\nmechanisms AS (\n  SELECT f.extraction, f.report_id, f.term\n  FROM   eval_findings f\n  WHERE  f.term IN (SELECT t.term FROM eval_kg_terms t WHERE t.kind = 'mechanism')\n),\nchecked AS (\n  SELECT pr.extraction,\n         pr.report_id,\n         p.pattern,\n         p.diagnosis_code,\n         p.min_strong,\n         p.urgency_hours,\n         (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM eval_findings f JOIN eval_kg_indicators i ON i.finding = f.term\n          WHERE  f.extraction = pr.extraction AND f.report_id = pr.report_id\n          AND    i.pattern = p.pattern AND i.role = 'strong')                      AS strong_found,\n         (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM eval_findings f JOIN eval_kg_indicators i ON i.finding = f.term\n          WHERE  f.extraction = pr.extraction AND f.report_id = pr.report_id\n          AND    i.pattern = p.pattern AND i.role = 'against')                     AS against_found,\n         (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM mechanisms m\n          WHERE  m.extraction = pr.extraction AND m.report_id = pr.report_id)      AS mechanisms_named,\n         (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM mechanisms m\n          WHERE  m.extraction = pr.extraction AND m.report_id = pr.report_id\n          AND    m.term = p.mechanism)                                             AS mechanism_match\n  FROM   pairs pr\n  JOIN   eval_kg_patterns p ON p.pattern = pr.pattern\n)\nSELECT c.*,\n       CASE WHEN c.strong_found >= c.min_strong\n             AND c.against_found = 0\n             AND (c.mechanisms_named = 0 OR c.mechanism_match > 0)\n            THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS fits\nFROM   checked c;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "#### How often does each pattern fit?\n\nPer extraction and pattern: in how many reports at least one indicator was found, how often the pattern fits and how often a counter-indicator contradicts it."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT extraction,\n       pattern,\n       COUNT(*)                                       AS reports_with_indicator,\n       SUM(fits)                                      AS fits,\n       COUNT(CASE WHEN against_found > 0 THEN 1 END)  AS contradicted\nFROM   eval_rule_check_v\nGROUP  BY extraction, pattern\nORDER  BY extraction, pattern;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 5. Predictions of all methods\n\nNow every method makes its diagnosis for every report.\n\n**Question:** What does each method predict?\n\n**How the query answers it:**\n\n`EVAL_PREDICTIONS` holds one row per method and report – or none, when a rule-based method abstains. The combined methods add up scores per candidate diagnosis:\n\n- **votes:** share of the 10 neighbours with this diagnosis (hybrid: of the 20 neighbours from both lists)\n- **knowledge graph:** `w_fit` if the pattern of the diagnosis fits, minus `w_against` if it has a counter-indicator (findings from the text index)\n- **spatial:** `w_terrain` if the report location lies in the typical terrain of the pattern\n\nThe diagnosis with the highest score wins."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "DROP TABLE IF EXISTS eval_predictions PURGE;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "CREATE TABLE eval_predictions (\n  method          VARCHAR2(40) NOT NULL,\n  report_id       VARCHAR2(30) NOT NULL,\n  predicted_code  VARCHAR2(60) NOT NULL,\n  CONSTRAINT pk_eval_predictions PRIMARY KEY (method, report_id)\n);"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "### M0 to M3: baseline and similarity votes"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "INSERT INTO eval_predictions (method, report_id, predicted_code)\nSELECT 'M0 majority', e.report_id, (SELECT STATS_MODE(true_code) FROM eval_cases)\nFROM   eval_cases e;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "INSERT INTO eval_predictions (method, report_id, predicted_code)\nSELECT method, report_id, neighbor_code\nFROM   (SELECT CASE retrieval WHEN 'vector' THEN 'M1 vector' ELSE 'M2 full text' END AS method,\n               report_id,\n               neighbor_code,\n               ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY retrieval, report_id\n                                  ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC, MIN(rank_no)) AS rn\n        FROM   eval_neighbors\n        GROUP  BY retrieval, report_id, neighbor_code)\nWHERE  rn = 1;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "INSERT INTO eval_predictions (method, report_id, predicted_code)\nSELECT 'M3 hybrid', report_id, neighbor_code\nFROM   (SELECT report_id,\n               neighbor_code,\n               ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY report_id\n                                  ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC, MIN(rank_no)) AS rn\n        FROM   eval_neighbors\n        GROUP  BY report_id, neighbor_code)\nWHERE  rn = 1;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "### M4 and M5: rules of the knowledge graph (they may abstain)"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "INSERT INTO eval_predictions (method, report_id, predicted_code)\nSELECT CASE extraction WHEN 'exact' THEN 'M4 ontology' ELSE 'M5 ontology + full text' END,\n       report_id,\n       diagnosis_code\nFROM   (SELECT r.*,\n               ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY r.extraction, r.report_id\n                                  ORDER BY r.strong_found + r.mechanism_match DESC, r.urgency_hours) AS rn\n        FROM   eval_rule_check_v r\n        WHERE  r.fits = 1)\nWHERE  rn = 1;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "### M6 to M8: GraphRAG, spatial and everything together"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "INSERT INTO eval_predictions (method, report_id, predicted_code)\nWITH votes_vector AS (\n  SELECT report_id, neighbor_code AS code, COUNT(*) / 10 AS score\n  FROM   eval_neighbors\n  WHERE  retrieval = 'vector'\n  GROUP  BY report_id, neighbor_code\n),\nvotes_hybrid AS (\n  SELECT report_id, neighbor_code AS code, COUNT(*) / 20 AS score\n  FROM   eval_neighbors\n  GROUP  BY report_id, neighbor_code\n),\nknowledge AS (\n  SELECT report_id,\n         diagnosis_code AS code,\n         (SELECT value FROM eval_settings WHERE param = 'w_fit') * fits - (SELECT value FROM eval_settings WHERE param = 'w_against') * SIGN(against_found) AS score\n  FROM   eval_rule_check_v\n  WHERE  extraction = 'text'\n),\nterrain AS (\n  SELECT e.report_id, p.diagnosis_code AS code, (SELECT value FROM eval_settings WHERE param = 'w_terrain') AS score\n  FROM   eval_cases e\n  JOIN   eval_kg_patterns p ON p.terrain = e.terrain\n),\ncomponents AS (\n  SELECT 'M6 GraphRAG' AS method, report_id, code, score FROM votes_vector\n  UNION ALL SELECT 'M6 GraphRAG',          report_id, code, score FROM knowledge\n  UNION ALL SELECT 'M7 GraphRAG + spatial', report_id, code, score FROM votes_vector\n  UNION ALL SELECT 'M7 GraphRAG + spatial', report_id, code, score FROM knowledge\n  UNION ALL SELECT 'M7 GraphRAG + spatial', report_id, code, score FROM terrain\n  UNION ALL SELECT 'M8 all',               report_id, code, score FROM votes_hybrid\n  UNION ALL SELECT 'M8 all',               report_id, code, score FROM knowledge\n  UNION ALL SELECT 'M8 all',               report_id, code, score FROM terrain\n)\nSELECT method, report_id, code\nFROM   (SELECT method, report_id, code,\n               ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY method, report_id ORDER BY SUM(score) DESC, code) AS rn\n        FROM   components\n        GROUP  BY method, report_id, code)\nWHERE  rn = 1;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "COMMIT;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 6. Results\n\n### 6a. Accuracy per method – the main table for the slide\n\n**Result** (run of 10.11.2026, 360 reports):\n\n| Method | Accuracy | Coverage | ACL recall |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| M0 majority (always sprain) | 31.9 % | 100 % | 0 % |\n| M1 vector | 50.6 % | 100 % | 30.0 % |\n| M2 full text | 59.4 % | 100 % | 57.5 % |\n| M3 hybrid | 60.0 % | 100 % | 46.3 % |\n| M4 ontology | 18.9 % | 23 % | 21.3 % |\n| M5 ontology + full text | 37.5 % | 43 % | 41.3 % |\n| M6 GraphRAG | 62.5 % | 100 % | 62.5 % |\n| M7 GraphRAG + spatial | 66.7 % | 100 % | 67.5 % |\n| M8 all | **70.8 %** | 100 % | **72.5 %** |\n\n- **Every single building block has a blind spot.** The vector search (M1) finds only 3 of 10 cruciate ligament ruptures: its neighbours sound like the patient, not like the findings (as in 11c). Full text (M2) does better here because the patients' telling words – *Knall*, *geknallt*, *Plopp* – are exact signals.\n- **Hybrid (M3) is no shortcut.** Fusion lifts accuracy only slightly above full text and lowers the ACL recall – as in 11d, one ranking is not a judgement.\n- **The ontology alone (M4) rarely answers** (23 % coverage): patients do not write the textbook terms. **With the text index (M5)** it answers almost twice as often – the same knowledge, found in more words.\n- **Each further combination adds:** GraphRAG (M6) 62.5 %, plus the location (M7) 66.7 %, everything together (M8) 70.8 %. The ACL recall rises from 30 % (vector alone) to 72.5 % – the dangerous injury is found 2.4 times as often.\n\nThe offline calibration of the generator had expected higher values for the vector search (≈ 66 %); the in-database model `ALL_MINILM_L12_V2` captures German colloquial findings less well. The main result is the same: every combination with the knowledge graph adds, and all building blocks together are best."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "WITH totals AS (\n  SELECT true_code, COUNT(*) AS n\n  FROM   eval_cases\n  GROUP  BY true_code\n),\nhits AS (\n  SELECT p.method,\n         e.true_code,\n         COUNT(*)                                                    AS answered,\n         COUNT(CASE WHEN p.predicted_code = e.true_code THEN 1 END)  AS correct\n  FROM   eval_predictions p\n  JOIN   eval_cases e ON e.report_id = p.report_id\n  GROUP  BY p.method, e.true_code\n)\nSELECT h.method,\n       ROUND(SUM(h.correct)  / (SELECT SUM(n) FROM totals) * 100, 1)                          AS accuracy_pct,\n       ROUND(SUM(h.answered) / (SELECT SUM(n) FROM totals) * 100)                             AS coverage_pct,\n       ROUND(SUM(CASE WHEN h.true_code = 'vkb_ruptur' THEN h.correct ELSE 0 END)\n             / (SELECT n FROM totals WHERE true_code = 'vkb_ruptur') * 100, 1)                 AS acl_recall_pct,\n       ROUND(SUM(h.correct / t.n) / (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM totals) * 100, 1)                    AS macro_recall_pct\nFROM   hits h\nJOIN   totals t ON t.true_code = h.true_code\nGROUP  BY h.method\nORDER  BY h.method;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "### 6b. Recall per diagnosis\n\n**Question:** For which diagnoses does each building block help?\n\n**How the query answers it:** The same predictions, broken down by true diagnosis (share of correctly recognised reports in %). This shows *where* each building block helps.\n\n**Result:** the vector search recognises 82 % of the sprains but only 30 % of the ruptures – it follows the most frequent diagnosis. The knowledge graph (M6) lifts the rupture from 30 % to 63 % and the medial ligament strain to 75 %. The location (M7) adds most for contusions (37 → 49 %), meniscus lesions (41 → 52 %) and ruptures (63 → 68 %). With everything together (M8), every injury reaches at least 56 %. The price: the sprain drops from 82 % to 77 % – a few harmless sprains are now taken for something more serious, while 34 more of the 80 ruptures are recognised (58 instead of 24). For a patient, that is the right trade."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "WITH totals AS (\n  SELECT true_code, COUNT(*) AS n\n  FROM   eval_cases\n  GROUP  BY true_code\n),\nhits AS (\n  SELECT p.method,\n         e.true_code,\n         COUNT(CASE WHEN p.predicted_code = e.true_code THEN 1 END) AS correct\n  FROM   eval_predictions p\n  JOIN   eval_cases e ON e.report_id = p.report_id\n  GROUP  BY p.method, e.true_code\n)\nSELECT *\nFROM   (SELECT h.method, h.true_code, ROUND(h.correct / t.n * 100) AS recall_pct\n        FROM   hits h\n        JOIN   totals t ON t.true_code = h.true_code)\nPIVOT  (MAX(recall_pct)\n        FOR true_code IN ('kniedistorsion' AS sprain, 'vkb_ruptur' AS acl, 'innenmeniskusläsion' AS meniscus,\n                          'innenbandzerrung' AS mcl, 'knieprellung' AS contusion))\nORDER  BY method;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "### 6c. The effect of the location\n\n**Question:** Does the location improve the diagnosis?\n\n**How the query answers it:** Only reports from the app have a location. On this subset the spatial evidence can make a difference; on the reports from practices M6 and M7 are identical by design.\n\n**Result:** on the 138 reports without a location, M6 and M7 both reach 63.0 % – as designed. On the 222 reports with a location, the location lifts GraphRAG from 62.2 % to 68.9 %, and M8 reaches 72.5 %. Where the app knows where the injury happened, that knowledge is worth almost 7 percentage points."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT CASE WHEN e.terrain IS NULL THEN 'no location' ELSE 'with location' END AS subset,\n       p.method,\n       COUNT(*)                                                                         AS reports,\n       ROUND(AVG(CASE WHEN p.predicted_code = e.true_code THEN 100 ELSE 0 END), 1)      AS accuracy_pct\nFROM   eval_cases e\nJOIN   eval_predictions p ON p.report_id = e.report_id\nWHERE  p.method IN ('M3 hybrid', 'M6 GraphRAG', 'M7 GraphRAG + spatial', 'M8 all')\nGROUP  BY CASE WHEN e.terrain IS NULL THEN 'no location' ELSE 'with location' END, p.method\nORDER  BY subset, p.method;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "### 6d. Fixed and broken: what each step changes\n\n**Question:** What does each additional building block fix – and what does it break?\n\n**How the query answers it:** For each step from one method to the next: how many reports become correct (*fixed*), how many become wrong (*broken*). A good building block fixes much more than it breaks. The predictions are first turned into one row per report with one column per method (conditional aggregation), so each step is a simple comparison of two columns.\n\n**Result:** every step fixes clearly more than it breaks. The knowledge graph on top of the vector votes (M1 → M6) fixes 63 reports and breaks 20 – net +43. Finding the terms with the text index (M4 → M5) fixes 68 and breaks just 1. The location (M6 → M7) fixes 22 and breaks 7, the hybrid votes (M7 → M8) fix 24 and break 9 – net +15 each. Adding full text to the vector votes (M1 → M3) fixes most, 56, but also breaks 22."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "WITH wide AS (\n  SELECT e.report_id,\n         e.true_code,\n         MAX(CASE WHEN p.method = 'M1 vector'               THEN p.predicted_code END) AS m1,\n         MAX(CASE WHEN p.method = 'M3 hybrid'               THEN p.predicted_code END) AS m3,\n         MAX(CASE WHEN p.method = 'M4 ontology'             THEN p.predicted_code END) AS m4,\n         MAX(CASE WHEN p.method = 'M5 ontology + full text' THEN p.predicted_code END) AS m5,\n         MAX(CASE WHEN p.method = 'M6 GraphRAG'             THEN p.predicted_code END) AS m6,\n         MAX(CASE WHEN p.method = 'M7 GraphRAG + spatial'   THEN p.predicted_code END) AS m7,\n         MAX(CASE WHEN p.method = 'M8 all'                  THEN p.predicted_code END) AS m8\n  FROM   eval_cases e\n  LEFT   JOIN eval_predictions p ON p.report_id = e.report_id\n  GROUP  BY e.report_id, e.true_code\n)\nSELECT 'add full text' AS step, 'M1 → M3' AS methods,\n       COUNT(CASE WHEN NVL(m1, '-') <> true_code AND m3 = true_code THEN 1 END) AS fixed,\n       COUNT(CASE WHEN m1 = true_code AND NVL(m3, '-') <> true_code THEN 1 END) AS broken\nFROM   wide\nUNION ALL\nSELECT 'add knowledge graph', 'M1 → M6',\n       COUNT(CASE WHEN NVL(m1, '-') <> true_code AND m6 = true_code THEN 1 END),\n       COUNT(CASE WHEN m1 = true_code AND NVL(m6, '-') <> true_code THEN 1 END)\nFROM   wide\nUNION ALL\nSELECT 'find terms with the text index', 'M4 → M5',\n       COUNT(CASE WHEN NVL(m4, '-') <> true_code AND m5 = true_code THEN 1 END),\n       COUNT(CASE WHEN m4 = true_code AND NVL(m5, '-') <> true_code THEN 1 END)\nFROM   wide\nUNION ALL\nSELECT 'add location', 'M6 → M7',\n       COUNT(CASE WHEN NVL(m6, '-') <> true_code AND m7 = true_code THEN 1 END),\n       COUNT(CASE WHEN m6 = true_code AND NVL(m7, '-') <> true_code THEN 1 END)\nFROM   wide\nUNION ALL\nSELECT 'hybrid instead of vector votes', 'M7 → M8',\n       COUNT(CASE WHEN NVL(m7, '-') <> true_code AND m8 = true_code THEN 1 END),\n       COUNT(CASE WHEN m7 = true_code AND NVL(m8, '-') <> true_code THEN 1 END)\nFROM   wide;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "### 6e. Examples for the slides: ruptures the vector search missed\n\n**Question:** Which ruptures did the vector search miss, and the combination find?\n\n**How the query answers it:** Cruciate ligament ruptures that M1 classified wrongly and M8 correctly: the text, what the neighbours voted and which pattern the knowledge graph found. These are the \"Jonas cases\" of the test set.\n\n**Result:** the first eight of these cases (by report ID). The vector search called seven of them a sprain, one a medial ligament strain. Five contain two strong indicators in words the graph knows – *Plopp*, *geknallt*, *geschnappt*, *gibt nach*, *wackelig*, *rasche Schwellung* – and the ACL pattern fits. Three are rescued without a fitting pattern: the hybrid votes and the demanding path tip the balance.\n\nThese three also show where the graph can learn: *\"Es hat laut gekracht\"* (P0278) and *\"das Knie hält nicht\"* (P0277) are not among its synonyms, and in P0300 one of the two indicators is not recognised either. Adding such phrases as `skos:altLabel` to `KNEE_KG` improves the evaluation without changing a line of SQL."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT e.report_id,\n       e.terrain,\n       v.predicted_code                                     AS vector_says,\n       a.predicted_code                                     AS all_say,\n       (SELECT LISTAGG(r.pattern, ', ') WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY r.pattern)\n        FROM   eval_rule_check_v r\n        WHERE  r.report_id = e.report_id AND r.extraction = 'text' AND r.fits = 1) AS fitting_patterns,\n       s.report_text\nFROM   eval_cases e\nJOIN   symptom_reports s  ON s.report_id = e.report_id\nJOIN   eval_predictions v ON v.report_id = e.report_id AND v.method = 'M1 vector'\nJOIN   eval_predictions a ON a.report_id = e.report_id AND a.method = 'M8 all'\nWHERE  e.true_code = 'vkb_ruptur'\nAND    v.predicted_code <> e.true_code\nAND    a.predicted_code =  e.true_code\nORDER  BY e.report_id\nFETCH  FIRST 8 ROWS ONLY;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 7. Spatial for the answer: is the recommendation feasible?\n\nA correct diagnosis does not help yet; Jonas needs a radiology he can reach.\n\n**Question:** Is the recommended radiology within reach?\n\n**How the query answers it:** A diagnosis alone does not help Jonas; he needs a radiology he can reach. For every report in the test set with a location whose true diagnosis needs an MRI (cruciate ligament rupture, meniscus tear) the query compares two strategies:\n\n- **graph only:** the radiology with the earliest free knee MRI slot in the whole care network after Monday noon (`GRAPH_TABLE`), the same for everybody\n- **graph + spatial:** the nearest radiology with an MRI device (`SDO_GEOM.SDO_DISTANCE` from the report location)\n\nA recommendation is **feasible** if the radiology is at most `feasible_km` (30 km) away in a straight line.\n\n**Result:** 84 reports need an MRI and have a location (52 ruptures, 32 meniscus lesions). The earliest slot is at *Radiologie Nürnberg Nord* – on average 32.6 km away, within 30 km for only 34.5 % of the patients. The nearest radiology with an MRI is on average 15.7 km away and within reach for all of them (100 %). The graph knows who is free first, the location knows who can get there – only together does the recommendation fit the patient (pattern M2)."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "WITH earliest AS (\n  SELECT g.provider_id\n  FROM   GRAPH_TABLE (care_network\n           MATCH (rad IS provider) -[o IS offers]-> (s IS slot WHERE s.status = 'frei' AND s.slot_type = 'MRT Knie'),\n                 (rad) -[op IS operates]-> (d IS device WHERE d.modality = 'MRT')\n           WHERE s.slot_start >= TIMESTAMP '2026-11-16 12:00:00 Europe/Berlin'\n           COLUMNS (rad.provider_id AS provider_id, s.slot_start AS slot_start)) g\n  ORDER  BY g.slot_start, g.provider_id\n  FETCH  FIRST 1 ROW ONLY\n),\nmri_cases AS (\n  SELECT e.report_id, s.geom\n  FROM   eval_cases e\n  JOIN   symptom_reports s ON s.report_id = e.report_id\n  WHERE  e.terrain IS NOT NULL\n  AND    e.true_code IN ('vkb_ruptur', 'innenmeniskusläsion')\n),\ngraph_only AS (\n  SELECT c.report_id,\n         SDO_GEOM.SDO_DISTANCE(c.geom,\n                               (SELECT p.geom FROM providers p WHERE p.provider_id = (SELECT provider_id FROM earliest)),\n                               0.05, 'unit=KM') AS km\n  FROM   mri_cases c\n),\ngraph_spatial AS (\n  SELECT c.report_id, n.km\n  FROM   mri_cases c\n  OUTER  APPLY (SELECT SDO_GEOM.SDO_DISTANCE(c.geom, p.geom, 0.05, 'unit=KM') AS km\n                FROM   providers p\n                WHERE  EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM devices d WHERE d.provider_id = p.provider_id AND d.modality = 'MRT')\n                ORDER  BY 1\n                FETCH  FIRST 1 ROW ONLY) n\n),\nstrategies AS (\n  SELECT 'graph only' AS strategy, report_id, km FROM graph_only\n  UNION ALL\n  SELECT 'graph + spatial',        report_id, km FROM graph_spatial\n),\nflagged AS (\n  SELECT s.strategy,\n         s.km,\n         CASE WHEN s.km <= (SELECT value FROM eval_settings WHERE param = 'feasible_km') THEN 100 ELSE 0 END AS feasible\n  FROM   strategies s\n),\nsummary AS (\n  SELECT strategy,\n         COUNT(*)                AS reports,\n         ROUND(AVG(km), 1)       AS avg_km,\n         ROUND(AVG(feasible), 1) AS feasible_pct\n  FROM   flagged\n  GROUP  BY strategy\n)\nSELECT m.strategy,\n       CASE m.strategy\n         WHEN 'graph only' THEN (SELECT p.name FROM providers p WHERE p.provider_id = (SELECT provider_id FROM earliest))\n         ELSE 'nearest with MRI'\n       END                       AS radiology,\n       m.reports,\n       m.avg_km,\n       m.feasible_pct\nFROM   summary m\nORDER  BY m.strategy DESC;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 8. GraphRAG with an LLM (optional): Vector RAG versus GraphRAG for Jonas\n\nMany applications hand the retrieved context to a large language model.\n\n**Question:** Does the model answer better with the knowledge graph in its context than with similar reports alone?\n\n**How the query answers it:** The same question twice to a large language model, with two different contexts built in SQL:\n\n- **Vector RAG:** the five reports most similar to Jonas' report with their diagnoses\n- **GraphRAG:** the same five reports, plus the check of the knowledge graph (per pattern: strong indicators found and needed, counter-indicators, recommendation, urgency) and the path in the care network (consultation and MRI slot)\n\n`DBMS_VECTOR_CHAIN.UTL_TO_GENERATE_TEXT` sends the prompt to OCI Generative AI. Prerequisites (once, values are placeholders):\n\n1. An OCI API key for a user with access to OCI Generative AI.\n2. A credential in the demo schema:\n```sql\nBEGIN\n  DBMS_VECTOR.CREATE_CREDENTIAL(\n    credential_name => 'OCI_GENAI_CRED',\n    params          => JSON('{\"user_ocid\"        : \"<user OCID>\",\n                              \"tenancy_ocid\"     : \"<tenancy OCID>\",\n                              \"compartment_ocid\" : \"<compartment OCID>\",\n                              \"private_key\"      : \"<private key without header and footer>\",\n                              \"fingerprint\"      : \"<fingerprint>\"}'));\nEND;\n/\n```\n3. As ADMIN, network access for the demo user to the Generative AI endpoint (`DBMS_NETWORK_ACL_ADMIN.APPEND_HOST_ACE`, host e.g. `inference.generativeai.eu-frankfurt-1.oci.oraclecloud.com`).\n\nAdjust region, URL and model in the parameters of the next cell. LLM answers vary from run to run; for the talk, keep a screenshot.\n\n**What to look for:** prompt and answer are in German, like the data. With Vector RAG the model tends to answer *„wahrscheinlich eine Verstauchung – schonen und kühlen“*; with GraphRAG it names the cruciate ligament, the MRI (*MRT*) within 48 hours and the concrete appointments, and it can explain why."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "WITH similar AS (\n  SELECT s.report_text, d.diagnosis_label\n  FROM   symptom_reports s\n  JOIN   diagnoses d ON d.report_id = s.report_id\n  WHERE  s.source = 'fhir'\n  ORDER  BY VECTOR_DISTANCE(s.report_embedding,\n                            (SELECT j.report_embedding FROM symptom_reports j WHERE j.report_id = 'SR-2026-11-16-0001'),\n                            COSINE)\n  FETCH  FIRST 5 ROWS ONLY\n),\nvector_context AS (\n  SELECT LISTAGG('- \"' || report_text || '\" -> ' || diagnosis_label, CHR(10)) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY diagnosis_label) AS txt\n  FROM   similar\n),\ngraph_context AS (\n  SELECT 'Prüfung der Meldung im Wissensgraphen: '\n         || (SELECT LISTAGG(pattern_label || ': ' || strong_found || ' starke Indizien gefunden, ' || strong_needed\n                            || ' nötig, ' || counter_found || ' Gegenindizien; Empfehlung ' || recommendation\n                            || ' innerhalb von ' || urgency_hours || ' h', '; ') WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY pattern_label)\n             FROM   TABLE(SEM_MATCH(\n                      'PREFIX kg:   <https://doag2026.example/knee#>\n            PREFIX rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>\n            PREFIX skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#>\n                         SELECT ?pattern_label ?strong_needed ?recommendation ?urgency_hours\n                                (COUNT(DISTINCT ?strong) AS ?strong_found) (COUNT(DISTINCT ?counter) AS ?counter_found)\n                         WHERE {\n                           ?pattern a kg:InjuryPattern ; rdfs:label ?pattern_label ;\n                                    kg:minStrongIndicators ?strong_needed ; kg:urgencyHours ?urgency_hours ;\n                                    kg:recommends ?procedure .\n                           ?procedure rdfs:label ?recommendation . FILTER (lang(?recommendation) = \"de\")\n                           <https://doag2026.example/knee#Report_SR-2026-11-16-0001> kg:reportsFinding ?strong .\n                           ?pattern kg:hasStrongIndicator ?strong .\n                           OPTIONAL { <https://doag2026.example/knee#Report_SR-2026-11-16-0001> kg:reportsFinding ?counter .\n                                      ?pattern kg:argumentAgainst ?counter }\n                         }\n                         GROUP BY ?pattern_label ?strong_needed ?recommendation ?urgency_hours',\n                      SEM_MODELS('KNEE_KG'), NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, ' ', NULL, NULL,\n         'DOAG2026', 'RDF_NETWORK')))\n         || CHR(10) || 'Versorgungsnetz: '\n         || (SELECT 'Sprechstunde ' || specialist || ' am ' || TO_CHAR(consultation, 'DY DD.MM. HH24:MI', 'NLS_DATE_LANGUAGE=GERMAN') || ' Uhr'\n                    || ', MRT in der ' || radiology || ' am ' || TO_CHAR(mri_slot, 'DY DD.MM. HH24:MI', 'NLS_DATE_LANGUAGE=GERMAN') || ' Uhr'\n             FROM   GRAPH_TABLE (care_network\n                      MATCH (p IS patient WHERE p.patient_id = 'P0001')\n                            -[r1 IS care_relation     WHERE r1.relation_type = 'hat_hausarzt']->   (gp   IS provider)\n                            -[r2 IS provider_relation WHERE r2.relation_type = 'ueberweist_an']->  (spec IS provider)\n                            -[r3 IS provider_relation WHERE r3.relation_type = 'kooperiert_mit']-> (rad  IS provider),\n                            (spec) -[o1 IS offers]-> (s1 IS slot WHERE s1.status = 'frei' AND s1.slot_type = 'Sprechstunde'),\n                            (rad)  -[o2 IS offers]-> (s2 IS slot WHERE s2.status = 'frei' AND s2.slot_type = 'MRT Knie')\n                      WHERE s1.slot_start >= TIMESTAMP '2026-11-16 12:00:00 Europe/Berlin'\n                        AND s2.slot_start >  s1.slot_start\n                        AND s2.slot_start <  TIMESTAMP '2026-11-17 12:00:00 Europe/Berlin'\n                      COLUMNS (spec.name AS specialist, s1.slot_start AS consultation,\n                               rad.name  AS radiology,  s2.slot_start AS mri_slot))\n             ORDER  BY consultation, mri_slot\n             FETCH  FIRST 1 ROW ONLY) AS txt\n  FROM   dual\n),\nprompts AS (\n  SELECT 'Vector RAG' AS approach,\n         'Du unterstützt eine fiktive Demo, dies ist keine medizinische Beratung. Ein Wanderer meldet: \"'\n         || (SELECT report_text FROM symptom_reports WHERE report_id = 'SR-2026-11-16-0001') || '\"' || CHR(10)\n         || 'Ähnliche frühere Meldungen und ihre Diagnosen:' || CHR(10) || v.txt || CHR(10)\n         || 'Antworte auf Deutsch in drei Sätzen: Welche Verletzung ist am wahrscheinlichsten, und was soll er jetzt tun?' AS prompt\n  FROM   vector_context v\n  UNION ALL\n  SELECT 'GraphRAG',\n         'Du unterstützt eine fiktive Demo, dies ist keine medizinische Beratung. Ein Wanderer meldet: \"'\n         || (SELECT report_text FROM symptom_reports WHERE report_id = 'SR-2026-11-16-0001') || '\"' || CHR(10)\n         || 'Ähnliche frühere Meldungen und ihre Diagnosen:' || CHR(10) || v.txt || CHR(10)\n         || (SELECT g.txt FROM graph_context g) || CHR(10)\n         || 'Wenn der Wissensgraph und die ähnlichen Meldungen sich widersprechen, folge dem Wissensgraphen und begründe es. '\n         || 'Antworte auf Deutsch in drei Sätzen: Welche Verletzung ist am wahrscheinlichsten, und was soll er jetzt tun?'\n  FROM   vector_context v\n)\nSELECT p.approach,\n       DBMS_VECTOR_CHAIN.UTL_TO_GENERATE_TEXT(\n         TO_CLOB(p.prompt),\n         JSON('{\"provider\"        : \"ocigenai\",\n                \"credential_name\" : \"OCI_GENAI_CRED\",\n                \"url\"             : \"https://inference.generativeai.eu-frankfurt-1.oci.oraclecloud.com/20231130/actions/chat\",\n                \"model\"           : \"meta.llama-3.3-70b-instruct\",\n                \"chatRequest\"     : {\"maxTokens\" : 300, \"temperature\" : 0}}')) AS answer,\n       p.prompt\nFROM   prompts p;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## Summary\n\n- Each building block finds a different kind of signal: **embeddings** similar meaning, the **full-text index** the variants of known terms, the **ontology** the decisive findings and rules, **spatial** the context of the place.\n- Alone, every block has a blind spot. **Combined in one database and one SQL statement**, they give the best diagnosis – above all for the dangerous, rarer injury – and spatial turns it into a recommendation the patient can actually follow.\n- For the slides: table 6a as a bar chart (accuracy and ACL recall per method), the fixed/broken counts of 6d and one example from 6e. Always add: *synthetic demo data*.\n\n### Clean-up (optional)\n\n```sql\nDROP VIEW  IF EXISTS eval_rule_check_v;\nDROP TABLE IF EXISTS eval_predictions PURGE;\nDROP TABLE IF EXISTS eval_neighbors PURGE;\nDROP TABLE IF EXISTS eval_findings PURGE;\nDROP TABLE IF EXISTS eval_term_queries PURGE;\nDROP TABLE IF EXISTS eval_kg_indicators PURGE;\nDROP TABLE IF EXISTS eval_kg_patterns PURGE;\nDROP TABLE IF EXISTS eval_kg_terms PURGE;\nDROP TABLE IF EXISTS eval_cases PURGE;\nDROP TABLE IF EXISTS eval_settings PURGE;\n```"
    languageId: markdown
